I made a postcard, because I was so excited about the show.

660
00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:25.190
So I made a postcard to mail out.

661
00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:28.730
Because back in those days, you actually mailed out postcards as an announcement.

662
00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:29.310
Yeah.

663
00:29:29.590 --> 00:29:32.570
I did this when I did theater in L.A.

664
00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:32.870
Yeah.

665
00:29:33.110 --> 00:29:35.650
You would put, like, a picture of yourself.

666
00:29:35.650 --> 00:29:37.210
And there was, like, we had a quote.

667
00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:38.090
We were, like, L.A.

668
00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:39.510
Weekly pick of the week.

669
00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:39.850
Right.

670
00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:44.810
And then you would make a postcard, and you would mail that to all the casting directors in town.

671
00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:46.790
When they had offices, right?

672
00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:49.490
I did the same thing backstage weSt.

673
00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:49.930
Right?

674
00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:56.050
And it was, so I, it was funny, because they couldn't find the right outfit for me, and

675
00:29:56.050 --> 00:29:57.070
eventually they did.

676
00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:01.250
And so we did that, and then.

677
00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:03.230
This postcard is amazing.

678
00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.070
It was, that was the best shot.

679
00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:06.830
I don't even remember who took that.

680
00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:10.990
What's so great about this picture, and I'll put it in our Insta stories, you guys, it's

681
00:30:10.990 --> 00:30:12.130
Carly as Madge.

682
00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:19.150
You're dripping with sweat, and you look like you've just played a lot of basketball.

683
00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:20.830
You have a really miserable expression.

684
00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:22.850
It's the date and time of the episode.

685
00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:23.910
This is fantastic.

686
00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:25.770
It was so much fun.

687
00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:30.670
I remember, because I went into hair and makeup, and I think, I know you were there, and Katie

688
00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:31.110
was there.

689
00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:32.830
One of you was there, and Katie was there.

690
00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:35.970
And somebody looked up at me and said, don't expect much.

691
00:30:36.130 --> 00:30:37.010
And I was like, what?

692
00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:37.850
Oh, no.

693
00:30:38.050 --> 00:30:39.670
Phyllis and Katie were in there.

694
00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:41.430
And they were like, don't expect much.

695
00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:42.650
It's not that kind of show.

696
00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:43.910
And I was like, what do you mean?

697
00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:46.050
And it was like, there's no, there's no makeup.

698
00:30:46.370 --> 00:30:47.470
There's, there's like nada.

699
00:30:47.770 --> 00:30:50.090
And I was like, I'm okay, fine.

700
00:30:50.270 --> 00:30:51.570
I don't, this is me.

701
00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:52.510
I don't wear much.

702
00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:52.930
And they were like.

703
00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:55.590
I mean, Carly, you and I have the same hairstyle in this episode.

704
00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:56.030
I know.

705
00:30:56.170 --> 00:30:56.850
I know.

706
00:30:56.990 --> 00:30:58.150
They put it in a braid.

707
00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:00.090
We both just have a French braid and called it a day.

708
00:31:00.130 --> 00:31:01.170
Yeah, that was it.

709
00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:06.670
And I had the bangs back then, and, and they just kept getting sweatier and sweatier.

710
00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:10.550
And there was like, nobody cared about anything.

711
00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:16.030
The only mistake I made was, I think in the warehouse scene, I made the mistake of climbing

712
00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:17.770
the rolling stairs.

713
00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:18.770
Oh, yeah.

714
00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:19.870
In the first scene.

715
00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:23.330
And I think I had to do that 50 times.

716
00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:26.090
Oh, because they liked it and it had to match.

717
00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:29.310
It was like taking, it was like doing a stair master.

718
00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:31.790
So was that on your first day of shooting?

719
00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:35.870
And then you had to come back with the sore legs and play basketball.

720
00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:38.410
And Leslie's trailer.

721
00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:42.430
You know how we all had those honey wagons the very first episode?

722
00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:42.650
Yeah, yeah.

723
00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:44.410
And you decorated them for Halloween.

724
00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:45.450
It was so cute.

725
00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:46.650
Kate and I, yes.

726
00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:47.970
We just talked about that.

727
00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:49.950
We went to the 99 cent store and decorated everything.

728
00:31:49.950 --> 00:31:50.890
It was the cutest thing.

729
00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:53.810
Everybody had little booze and ghosts and everything.

730
00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:58.710
But Leslie's trailer was here and then Brian and then me or whatever.

731
00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:04.690
And I remember Leslie was sitting on his stairs and Brian was sitting on his.

732
00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:08.810
And I tried to get up into my trailer and I was like, ow.

733
00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:12.090
And Leslie looked at me and he goes, Arnica, Arnica.

734
00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:12.970
And I was like, what?

735
00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:15.970
And he goes, go get yourself some Arnica gel.

736
00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:17.070
It's the only thing.

737
00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:18.410
That and a hot bath.

738
00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:19.610
And I was like, okay.

739
00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:22.010
That is so Leslie, isn't it?

740
00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:22.890
A hot bath.

741
00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:24.090
Yeah, I love it.

742
00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:26.010
So many times you told us to take a hot bath.

743
00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:29.670
My legs hurt so bad after the third day and we were filming.

744
00:32:29.890 --> 00:32:33.610
And all I kept doing was running up and down the court, up and down.

745
00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:35.230
And we were on a concrete floor.

746
00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:35.710
Yeah.

747
00:32:36.670 --> 00:32:38.650
And my legs hurt so bad.

748
00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:41.790
It was like the third day and I was like, I couldn't walk anymore.

749
00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:44.330
Well, you know, we had the opposite problem.

750
00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:45.410
You were sitting.

751
00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:49.570
We sat on that metal bench with no cushion for three days.

752
00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:52.570
And my butt and legs kept falling asleep.

753
00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:58.430
Or that thing that happens where like I also couldn't walk because I was like stiff from

754
00:32:58.430 --> 00:33:01.330
like sitting in the same position for hours and hours.

755
00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:02.350
That's wow.

756
00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:04.910
Also, I mean, whatever, overshare.

757
00:33:05.030 --> 00:33:08.030
But that bench had these long grooved lines in it.

758
00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:08.590
Yes.

759
00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:13.430
And when you went, when I went home and I changed clothes, I had like waffle balls.

760
00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:13.790
Oh, no.

761
00:33:14.730 --> 00:33:18.890
But this is a really great tip for any aspiring actor out there.

762
00:33:19.150 --> 00:33:24.190
Whatever you do in the background on the first take, you will be doing over and over and over

763
00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:24.490
again.

764
00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:32.030
One of my friends had told me like a long time ago, never, ever, ever pick a tough job

765
00:33:32.030 --> 00:33:32.530
to do.

766
00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:38.390
Like because on one, I think of one commercial shoot, he made the mistake of choosing a jackhammer.

767
00:33:38.630 --> 00:33:39.170
Oh, no.

768
00:33:39.170 --> 00:33:41.250
I was like, really sorry he ever did that.

769
00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:46.210
And I'll never forget that because afterwards I was like, why did I choose to go up and down

770
00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:46.850
the stairs?

771
00:33:47.050 --> 00:33:50.930
Because I would, my business was to go up the stairs and get a box and bring it down or

772
00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:52.830
go up the stairs and put a box up on a shelf.

773
00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.950
I was like, what was I thinking?

774
00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:57.010
I couldn't move after.

775
00:33:57.070 --> 00:33:57.930
We've all done it.

776
00:33:57.930 --> 00:33:58.910
Oh, my God.

777
00:33:59.110 --> 00:33:59.390
We have.

778
00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:03.630
It was like the fourth day, I think it was, or the, it was the last day.

779
00:34:03.770 --> 00:34:06.210
Was it the last day of shooting when I fell?

780
00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:07.930
I think it was.

781
00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:12.970
It was, thankfully, it was the last day of shooting because you didn't have to come back.

782
00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:14.450
You fell and hurt your knee.

783
00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:14.890
I did.

784
00:34:14.890 --> 00:34:15.690
Really badly.

785
00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:18.150
Oh, and I didn't want anybody to know.

786
00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:19.870
I thought I was going to try and hide it.

787
00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:23.570
I was chasing Steve down court.

788
00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:27.410
He went tearing after, and I went running after him.

789
00:34:27.410 --> 00:34:31.070
And my legs were just, like, not working.

790
00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:32.930
They just stopped working.

791
00:34:32.930 --> 00:34:36.450
And I just went flying, and I landed on my left knee.

792
00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:37.630
On the concrete.

793
00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:38.870
Caught right on the concrete.

794
00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:44.390
And I got up, and I kept running, and I kept playing, and then they said, cut.

795
00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:50.170
And I immediately went back to the back where I think there was a cooler filled with ice

796
00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:51.530
and drinks or something like that.

797
00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:56.810
And I just very quickly grabbed a Ziploc baggie and filled it with ice.

798
00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:58.230
And didn't say anything.

799
00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:00.450
I just kind of sat there and sat down for a minute.

800
00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:02.310
And it was really hurting.

801
00:35:02.550 --> 00:35:06.230
And so I just kind of quietly came out, and I sat down on the bench next to you.

802
00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:07.950
And I had the ice pack on my knee.

803
00:35:08.310 --> 00:35:09.750
And I moved it for a second.

804
00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:12.130
And you looked down at my knee, and you went, oh, my God!

805
00:35:12.190 --> 00:35:13.690
And I was like, shh.

806
00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:19.610
I was going to say, my memory is that that thing swelled up faSt.

807
00:35:19.990 --> 00:35:20.350
It was huge.

808
00:35:20.510 --> 00:35:20.710
Yeah.

809
00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:26.030
And I kind of remember being like, you got to—we got to get the medic.

810
00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:26.350
Yeah.

811
00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:27.410
We got to tell somebody.

812
00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:27.670
Yeah.

813
00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:28.510
Like, don't—

814
00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:29.190
It didn't match.

815
00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:29.550
Try to keep going.

816
00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:32.330
It didn't—my memory was it didn't match your other knee at all.

817
00:35:32.410 --> 00:35:32.890
Not close.

818
00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:34.770
I was like, oh, my gosh.

819
00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:35.220
Yeah.

820
00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:37.230
And then, Jenna, I think you got the medic, didn't you?

821
00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:43.650
You got the medic.

822
00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.090
I'm like, I smell gas in the work bus.

823
00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:46.770
I know.

824
00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:47.250
Yeah.

825
00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:49.310
This entry needs attending to.

826
00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:49.490
Yeah.

827
00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:50.910
She was super sweet.

828
00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:54.390
And she took one look, and she said, no, we're going to do an accident report.

829
00:35:54.490 --> 00:35:58.550
And I was sitting back in the back, and I was like, I am so mortified.

830
00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:00.170
I didn't want to deal with this.

831
00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:00.790
It's fine.

832
00:36:00.790 --> 00:36:01.930
I'll be fine, blah, blah, blah.

833
00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:04.590
And she said, no, we're going to have you have that checked out.

834
00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:07.210
And they filled out the forms and did the whole thing.

835
00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:10.170
And I was mortified because I was like, oh, my God.

836
00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:11.530
I'm not—we're not done filming.

837
00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:17.270
And I'm—this is terrible, and blah, and I'd never hurt myself on a set before.

838
00:36:17.570 --> 00:36:23.230
And this was my first three-day job ever, three days of filming.

839
00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:26.750
That was like the first—I had done two other jobs.

840
00:36:26.810 --> 00:36:27.570
It's a big deal.

841
00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:28.530
It's a big deal.

842
00:36:28.610 --> 00:36:28.670
It was huge.

843
00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:35.270
I remember you being worried, like, will they ever bring me back because I got hurt?

844
00:36:35.430 --> 00:36:38.590
And that's such a thing when you're an actor starting out.

845
00:36:38.590 --> 00:36:44.770
Like, an accident happens, but you're afraid you'll get branded as difficult or, like, whatever.

846
00:36:45.030 --> 00:36:53.370
But that wasn't—that's—I mean, thankfully, I feel like, especially on The Office, that wasn't the tone of our set.

847
00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:53.790
Yeah.

848
00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:55.610
And you needed medical attention.

849
00:36:56.490 --> 00:36:58.450
And we did bring you back.

850
00:36:58.450 --> 00:36:58.730
Yeah.

851
00:36:58.730 --> 00:36:58.930
Yeah.

852
00:36:59.050 --> 00:36:59.750
It was all fine.

853
00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:01.550
It was—I was so grateful.

854
00:37:01.550 --> 00:37:08.850
Well, you know, I want you to know that in the superfan version on Peacock, all of your stuff with Dwight—

855
00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:13.710
imagine Dwight, like, it's like going down the court against each other is all in there.

856
00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:14.530
In your face.

857
00:37:14.610 --> 00:37:16.930
And clearly that happened before you hurt your knee.

858
00:37:17.030 --> 00:37:17.150
Right.

859
00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:17.770
And you know what?

860
00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:18.390
I'm so glad.

861
00:37:18.570 --> 00:37:21.570
Now people will see how hard you were busting your ass on that court.

862
00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:22.550
I was trying.

863
00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:23.430
Yeah.

864
00:37:23.430 --> 00:37:28.170
I was—I was the—who was the guy who choreographed the basketball game?

865
00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:29.450
There was somebody there.

866
00:37:29.450 --> 00:37:32.850
Oh, we had a stunt coordinator that choreographed the game.

867
00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:38.990
But then there was parts of it that, you know, you guys were just running back and forth and kind of just freestyling.

868
00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:44.250
But if you watch the superfan version, Madge and Dwight really square off a lot.

869
00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:45.150
What was that like?

870
00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:46.150
Because we know rain.

871
00:37:46.390 --> 00:37:47.710
Rain goes all out.

872
00:37:47.950 --> 00:37:48.070
Yeah.

873
00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:57.410
There was a stunt coordinator who actually showed me how it was going to work, and it was like a choreographed thing.

874
00:37:57.530 --> 00:37:57.830
Mm-hmm.

875
00:37:57.830 --> 00:38:00.990
And then when he grabs it from me, we're supposed to fight.

876
00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:04.870
And there were a couple times where I think we even ended up on the ground.

877
00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:05.570
Yeah.

878
00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:05.930
Yeah.

879
00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:06.670
You did.

880
00:38:06.810 --> 00:38:07.010
Yeah.

881
00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:12.950
And then I was like, I'm not letting go of the ball.

882
00:38:13.030 --> 00:38:14.730
I'm not letting go of the ball.

883
00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:17.370
And he got into it, and I got into it.

884
00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:18.390
We started fighting.

885
00:38:18.390 --> 00:38:23.710
And it was so much fun because he was like, I just got mad at him.

886
00:38:23.830 --> 00:38:25.030
I was like, back off, man.

887
00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:25.930
Just back off.

888
00:38:26.530 --> 00:38:28.630
And he goes, no, I'm not going to back off.

889
00:38:28.730 --> 00:38:30.730
And I'm going, get out of my face.

890
00:38:30.850 --> 00:38:32.770
And I was like, in your face.

891
00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:36.050
It was really good and Phil's very honeSt.

892
00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:42.070
But that whole storyline between Dwight and Madge reminded me of like that, you know,

893
00:38:42.990 --> 00:38:48.950
eight-year-old boy on like the schoolyard that you're playing kickball with who goes so super hard.

894
00:38:49.030 --> 00:38:49.150
Yeah.

895
00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:52.470
And you're like, buddy, relax, except it's a grown man.

896
00:38:52.670 --> 00:39:04.130
And if I hadn't hurt myself, we almost had this little secret thing that we were going to do behind the boxes when he takes a sip of water and he spills it all over his face.

897
00:39:04.130 --> 00:39:04.410
Yeah.

898
00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:12.110
Right before that, we were going to have this little secret thing behind the boxes, and that never came to fruition.

899
00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:13.010
What secret thing?

900
00:39:13.150 --> 00:39:13.930
Like Dwight and Madge hookup?

901
00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:14.850
Like a little rope.

902
00:39:14.950 --> 00:39:17.510
Oh, like this was all foreplay on the court.

903
00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:18.610
Oh, my gosh.

904
00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:21.730
He met his physical match and he was turned on.

905
00:39:22.550 --> 00:39:27.670
We were going to come up with something, and then it never happened, unfortunately.

906
00:39:28.270 --> 00:39:32.630
But as it turned out, obviously, it never needed to happen.

907
00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:34.670
Well, I think it would have worked.

908
00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:39.770
And you know what would have been great is if Angela found out and then really, like then Angela hated Madge.

909
00:39:39.810 --> 00:39:40.430
Oh, my gosh.

910
00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:46.990
Well, speaking of scenes in the warehouse where things were added, I want to jump to the boys and girls episode.

911
00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:53.750
Because Dave Rogers wants you to know that he put an extra talking head of yours into boys and girls.

912
00:39:54.070 --> 00:40:00.510
This is when the documentary crew is filming down in the warehouse and they find Madge pushing a cart of supplies.

913
00:40:00.850 --> 00:40:02.130
It's very funny.

914
00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:03.190
We're going to play it.

915
00:40:06.450 --> 00:40:08.610
I'm wanted in three states.

916
00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:14.790
Did I say don't videotape me?

917
00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:15.530
Don't tape me.

918
00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:16.130
Yeah.

919
00:40:16.470 --> 00:40:18.470
I'm wanted in three states?

920
00:40:18.470 --> 00:40:20.070
What is she wanted for?

921
00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:21.270
Oh, my God.

922
00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:23.730
That's hysterical.

923
00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:25.450
Do you even remember doing that?

924
00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:29.150
I feel like that must have been an improv moment.

925
00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:29.630
Totally.

926
00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:30.170
Right?

927
00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:32.610
Or something they fed you on the day.

928
00:40:33.330 --> 00:40:40.490
They were capturing little moments in the warehouse because right before that is Lonnie and they're filming him.

929
00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:44.710
He's got that giant plastic like saran wrap and he's wrapping it around the boxes.

930
00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:47.670
And he's like, sometimes I pretend to be a car.

931
00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:55.030
And then it cuts to you and you're pushing the cart and you're surly from the get go.

932
00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:58.890
So, you have to check out some of these super fans because there's more Madge.

933
00:40:59.290 --> 00:40:59.650
Mm-hmm.

934
00:40:59.890 --> 00:41:03.530
We also want to ask you about safety training.

935
00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:04.770
Oh, my God.

936
00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:09.150
So, this is the episode where Madge gets her nickname from Michael.

937
00:41:09.270 --> 00:41:09.710
Pudge.

938
00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:10.230
Mm-hmm.

939
00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:10.690
Yes.

940
00:41:10.830 --> 00:41:12.590
You are so funny in that scene.

941
00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:14.910
Do you know, I found the shooting script for that.

942
00:41:15.070 --> 00:41:15.230
Mm-hmm.

943
00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:17.550
That one I saved on my computer.

944
00:41:18.950 --> 00:41:20.950
First of all, Harold Ramis.

945
00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:21.530
What?

946
00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:22.090
I know.

947
00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:23.030
What?

948
00:41:23.370 --> 00:41:23.870
I know.

949
00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:32.610
When I saw that, I almost jumped out of my skin because Avery Schreiber was my mentor from Second

950
00:41:32.610 --> 00:41:32.950
City.

951
00:41:33.190 --> 00:41:40.430
And Avery and Harold and Meine Kolb and Severin Darden, all the original OGs from Second

952
00:41:40.430 --> 00:41:48.670
City, were all, I think Harold told me this, at one of the fabulous lunches that we always

953
00:41:48.670 --> 00:41:49.610
had together.

954
00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:51.350
Yes, at the big, long tables.

955
00:41:51.670 --> 00:41:52.010
Yes.

956
00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:58.750
That's one of the things I love about this cast is that every time from the first week

957
00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:02.130
when we shot basketball, we all sat down family style.

958
00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:02.590
Mm-hmm.

959
00:42:02.590 --> 00:42:09.090
I mean, the first week I had dinner with you guys, I sat down with John Krasinski and his

960
00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:09.590
parents.

961
00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:10.160
Yeah.

962
00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:10.930
Do you remember that?

963
00:42:11.050 --> 00:42:12.730
He brought his parents to that episode.

964
00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:13.150
Yeah.

965
00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:14.530
And I was like, oh, my God.

966
00:42:14.530 --> 00:42:18.170
And Harold sat there telling Second City stories.

967
00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:18.550
Yeah.

968
00:42:18.670 --> 00:42:22.010
And it was Harold and BJ and me and Phyllis and you.

969
00:42:22.230 --> 00:42:25.470
I mean, it was like, I'm having lunch with Harold Ramis.

970
00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:26.490
What is happening?

971
00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:27.130
No.

972
00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:30.990
And he told Second City stories that were just irreplaceable.

973
00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:35.110
So that was a gift to have that moment.

974
00:42:35.110 --> 00:42:41.410
And in the safety training episode, there were no—God bless BJ Novak.

975
00:42:41.510 --> 00:42:43.690
He wrote me into the episodes I was in.

976
00:42:43.750 --> 00:42:45.170
It was because he wrote me in.

977
00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:46.950
I didn't have any lines.

978
00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:49.410
And we were just all standing there.

979
00:42:49.590 --> 00:42:51.430
And then he said, her.

980
00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:54.570
Her is qualified to ride the forklift.

981
00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:56.730
And Lonnie kind of nudged me.

982
00:42:56.830 --> 00:42:57.730
And I went, Madge.

983
00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:00.590
It's Madge.

984
00:43:00.910 --> 00:43:04.170
And Steve looked at me and he goes, I thought your name was Pudge.

985
00:43:04.250 --> 00:43:04.890
And I'm like, no.

986
00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:06.230
It's always been Madge.

987
00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:08.870
And I looked over and you guys were all busting up.

988
00:43:08.950 --> 00:43:09.450
And I was like—

989
00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:10.410
It was so funny.

990
00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:11.430
So funny.

991
00:43:11.770 --> 00:43:15.870
Well, you know, in Boys and Girls, one of my favorite moments is the character reveal

992
00:43:15.870 --> 00:43:18.250
that you're there because it's just the men in a circle.

993
00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:18.970
Guys gripe session.

994
00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:19.270
Yeah.

995
00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:19.610
Yeah.

996
00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:21.310
And then you lean forward.

997
00:43:21.510 --> 00:43:22.970
And there you are.

998
00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:25.850
And it busts me up every time I watch it.

999
00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:27.470
Denny snuck that one.

1000
00:43:27.610 --> 00:43:28.390
I love her.

1001
00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:29.990
She was so much fun.

1002
00:43:30.210 --> 00:43:31.870
We were all sitting on boxes.

1003
00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:34.730
And she was like, hide behind David and John.

1004
00:43:35.210 --> 00:43:36.590
And I was like, what?

1005
00:43:36.910 --> 00:43:37.410
Okay.

1006
00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:44.610
And then I think somehow or another, David, out of the corner of his eye, looks back at

1007
00:43:44.610 --> 00:43:44.870
me.

1008
00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:50.850
Or he looks out of the corner of his eye to, like, indicate to Michael, there's somebody

1009
00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:51.490
else here.

1010
00:43:51.790 --> 00:43:52.010
Right.

1011
00:43:52.010 --> 00:43:53.250
You're getting it wrong.

1012
00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:59.290
And then I just kind of leaned forward and I was like, hey, you want me to go?

1013
00:43:59.710 --> 00:44:06.550
And then Steve got this look on his face like, no, why would I want you to go?

1014
00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:10.430
And I was like, because I don't belong here, you moron.

1015
00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:15.270
Why am I not upstairs with the women?

1016
00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:16.350
What part of this?

1017
00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:18.470
I so wish Madge had come upstairs.

1018
00:44:18.910 --> 00:44:20.050
Wouldn't that have been so fun?

1019
00:44:20.110 --> 00:44:20.950
I would have loved that.

1020
00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:25.910
That would have been really, really fun to have you come upstairs after that.

1021
00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:29.970
And be part of the insane conversation we were having.

1022
00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:30.970
You guys were having.

1023
00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:31.370
Yeah.

1024
00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:31.930
I know.

1025
00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:32.930
That would have been wonderful.

1026
00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:34.310
But it was funny.

1027
00:44:34.470 --> 00:44:35.010
It was funny.

1028
00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:37.310
And Denny choreographed that really well.

1029
00:44:37.710 --> 00:44:38.390
She was just like—

1030
00:44:39.230 --> 00:44:43.990
And when I walk by him, the expression on his face was just priceless.

1031
00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:44.810
So wonderful.

1032
00:44:45.130 --> 00:44:46.250
It was just priceless.

1033
00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:47.110
That was fun.

1034
00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:48.470
That was very, very fun.

1035
00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:52.910
Can we also discuss some deleted scenes from Secret Santa?

1036
00:44:53.150 --> 00:44:53.430
Yes.

1037
00:44:53.630 --> 00:44:54.730
Do you remember these?

1038
00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:55.830
Do you know what I'm going to say?

1039
00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:57.130
My Garfield pasta?

1040
00:44:57.350 --> 00:44:57.790
Yes.

1041
00:44:58.950 --> 00:44:59.390
Yes.

1042
00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:03.750
Well, it's been added back to the Peacock Superfan episode.

1043
00:45:04.070 --> 00:45:06.030
Let me set it up, and then we're going to listen to it.

1044
00:45:06.150 --> 00:45:06.330
Okay.

1045
00:45:06.630 --> 00:45:09.730
So this is the party that Jim and Dwight are planning.

1046
00:45:10.070 --> 00:45:15.550
And Jim is nervous because the warehouse folks have now been invited to this party,

1047
00:45:15.590 --> 00:45:17.790
and they're worried that they're not going to have enough food.

1048
00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:21.830
So everybody's standing in line from the warehouse to get food.

1049
00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:29.450
And he says, it's just finger foods, kind of like, please don't take a lot, and one lasagna.

1050
00:45:30.470 --> 00:45:31.950
And then here's what happens.

1051
00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:35.770
So guys, this is more like finger food.

1052
00:45:35.890 --> 00:45:36.890
Uh-oh, a lasagna.

1053
00:45:37.070 --> 00:45:38.010
Look out, Garfield.

1054
00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:39.690
Uh-oh.

1055
00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:40.650
What does that mean?

1056
00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:42.030
Oh, they call me Garfield.

1057
00:45:42.190 --> 00:45:44.790
I've never seen a lasagna not eat in the whole pan.

1058
00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:45.930
Oh, I wish you wouldn't.

1059
00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:48.210
Oh, well, I can't help myself.

1060
00:45:48.210 --> 00:45:51.790
Can you imagine what it's like for me, that it has that power over me?

1061
00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:52.890
I've got to have some.

1062
00:45:53.350 --> 00:45:54.190
Sorry, guys.

1063
00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:55.430
Everybody get some?

1064
00:45:55.810 --> 00:45:56.710
I got mine.

1065
00:45:56.990 --> 00:45:57.130
Okay.

1066
00:46:00.280 --> 00:46:02.040
Okay, so that's the scene.

1067
00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:03.400
But then what happens?

1068
00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:05.760
We're outside of the conference room.

1069
00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:06.080
Yeah.

1070
00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:11.300
And inside the conference room, you are eating all of the rest of the lasagna,

1071
00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:13.920
and all the warehouse guys are screaming,

1072
00:46:14.020 --> 00:46:14.820
Garfield!

1073
00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:15.720
Garfield!

1074
00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:16.560
Garfield!

1075
00:46:16.800 --> 00:46:18.240
How much lasagna did you eat?

1076
00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:20.400
That's our question.

1077
00:46:20.400 --> 00:46:21.480
A half a pan.

1078
00:46:21.980 --> 00:46:22.340
Oh!

1079
00:46:22.520 --> 00:46:24.060
No, no, I had a spit bucket.

1080
00:46:24.240 --> 00:46:24.500
Okay.

1081
00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:25.280
I had a spit bucket.

1082
00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:30.020
They had three changes, shirt changes, which I didn't need because I was very careful.

1083
00:46:30.320 --> 00:46:32.020
But there was a spit bucket right there,

1084
00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:34.980
and I think you were the one that suggested that I needed one.

1085
00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:35.540
Thank you.

1086
00:46:35.820 --> 00:46:36.420
You know why?

1087
00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:38.760
Do you know how we all say, be careful what you do in the background?

1088
00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:39.120
Yeah.

1089
00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:43.480
Mine was the very first time we ate ice cream cake.

1090
00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:46.740
It was the Alliance, and it was Meredith's birthday.

1091
00:46:46.860 --> 00:46:47.260
Oh, right.

1092
00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:50.920
And I took a big old bite of that ice cream cake at seven in the morning.

1093
00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:52.140
I was so excited.

1094
00:46:52.600 --> 00:46:55.760
And then we ate it for hours, and I thought, that's it.

1095
00:46:55.980 --> 00:46:59.140
I can't—I still, to this day, like, I'm like, I don't want any ice cream cake.

1096
00:46:59.500 --> 00:46:59.560
Yeah.

1097
00:46:59.640 --> 00:46:59.800
No.

1098
00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:00.180
Yeah.

1099
00:47:00.340 --> 00:47:00.460
No.

1100
00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:01.300
Spit bucket.

1101
00:47:01.620 --> 00:47:02.400
And it was good.

1102
00:47:02.660 --> 00:47:07.400
I mean, it was delicious because whoever at Crafty made it, it was wonderful.

1103
00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:10.160
But after about the fourth time.

1104
00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:14.360
The fourth half of lasagna you ate.

1105
00:47:14.580 --> 00:47:15.800
The fourth half of lasagna I ate.

1106
00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:22.500
Well, because they left—I think whoever did it left me, like, in the tray, they left me, like, this quarter.

1107
00:47:23.120 --> 00:47:28.240
So I just had to really put the whole thing up and just scoop it in like that.

1108
00:47:28.280 --> 00:47:29.480
Yeah, because you're, like, shoving it in your mouth.

1109
00:47:29.480 --> 00:47:29.780
Mm-hmm.

1110
00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:30.060
Yeah.

1111
00:47:30.180 --> 00:47:33.640
And the whole quarter of the pan is up like this.

1112
00:47:34.500 --> 00:47:42.440
But the funny part was when I walked in, I had worked so hard on my lines because I wanted to get them right because this is my first scene with John.

1113
00:47:42.680 --> 00:47:44.200
And he completely improvised.

1114
00:47:44.520 --> 00:47:48.800
And I was like, and he said, oh, I'm sorry.

1115
00:47:49.460 --> 00:47:50.140
I'm sorry.

1116
00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:51.480
I was going to improvise something.

1117
00:47:51.620 --> 00:47:52.460
And I was like, no, that's fine.

1118
00:47:52.460 --> 00:47:54.940
I just was ready for something else.

1119
00:47:55.020 --> 00:47:56.680
And he said, no, I'll do the other thing.

1120
00:47:56.740 --> 00:48:01.560
I was like, okay, but I'm fine now that I know you were going to go off in a different direction.

1121
00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:02.380
He was like, no, no, no.

1122
00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:03.800
I'll stick to what we got.

1123
00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:05.120
I was like, sorry.

1124
00:48:05.740 --> 00:48:07.300
So I got really nervous after that.

1125
00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:10.420
And then Craig started chanting Garfield, Garfield.

1126
00:48:10.500 --> 00:48:11.760
And I was like, please don't do that.

1127
00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:12.520
I'm going to laugh.

1128
00:48:13.580 --> 00:48:15.580
I'm going to laugh and choke to death.

1129
00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:16.520
Please don't do that.

1130
00:48:16.520 --> 00:48:18.700
But then the spit bucket happened.

1131
00:48:18.860 --> 00:48:20.100
And then we shot it.

1132
00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:21.500
And I didn't get any shirts dirty.

1133
00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:23.140
And I was like, okay, I did that right.

1134
00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:23.700
Did that right.

1135
00:48:23.800 --> 00:48:24.520
Everything's cool.

1136
00:48:24.980 --> 00:48:26.320
And we finished shooting.

1137
00:48:26.680 --> 00:48:29.060
And then they said, okay, let's take a break.

1138
00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:29.820
Everybody go.

1139
00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:31.880
And, you know, Crafty set up lunch.

1140
00:48:32.040 --> 00:48:32.880
And I walk outside.

1141
00:48:33.600 --> 00:48:34.940
And I open the lid.

1142
00:48:34.940 --> 00:48:36.200
And they're serving lasagna.

1143
00:48:36.300 --> 00:48:38.540
And I go, oh, look, lasagna.

1144
00:48:38.840 --> 00:48:42.600
It was probably all the lasagnas we didn't use in the scene.

1145
00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:42.880
Yeah.

1146
00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:45.360
They're like, we know what we're going to do with the extra lasagna.

1147
00:48:45.640 --> 00:48:48.500
Somebody said, Carly gets quoted the day.

1148
00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.860
So I got quoted the day on the sheet.

1149
00:48:50.980 --> 00:48:51.660
On the call sheet.

1150
00:48:51.880 --> 00:48:52.380
On the call sheet.

1151
00:48:52.540 --> 00:48:52.840
Oh, yeah.

1152
00:48:53.140 --> 00:48:54.720
I was like, oh, no.

1153
00:48:54.920 --> 00:48:56.520
I didn't mean to be ungrateful.

1154
00:48:56.780 --> 00:48:58.320
But lasagna for lunch.

1155
00:48:58.500 --> 00:48:58.760
Yay.

1156
00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:01.680
No, everybody got it.

1157
00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:02.480
That was brilliant.

1158
00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:03.260
It was great.

1159
00:49:03.940 --> 00:49:05.100
I'm like, oh, no.

1160
00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:09.280
Well, I wanted to ask you about the day that we learned the scarn.

1161
00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:10.180
Do you remember that?

1162
00:49:10.220 --> 00:49:10.680
I do.

1163
00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:11.200
We all got to do the scarn.

1164
00:49:11.200 --> 00:49:13.680
We all had to practice in that side room.

1165
00:49:13.900 --> 00:49:14.300
Yes.

1166
00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:15.440
That was hysterical.

1167
00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:17.720
And I was so worried I wasn't going to get it right.

1168
00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:19.120
I can't dance, Carly.

1169
00:49:19.260 --> 00:49:20.480
I just can't.

1170
00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:22.340
It's like you have to really break it down for me.

1171
00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:23.780
I think you can see me counting.

1172
00:49:25.620 --> 00:49:27.320
But that was so fun.

1173
00:49:27.660 --> 00:49:28.700
That was hysterical.

1174
00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:30.620
They made me change my hair.

1175
00:49:30.980 --> 00:49:31.480
Mm-hmm.

1176
00:49:31.680 --> 00:49:33.300
Because I had a braid and you had a braid.

1177
00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:37.160
Even though in basketball, it was okay that we both had the same hair, I guess, for the

1178
00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:37.500
scarn.

1179
00:49:37.580 --> 00:49:38.800
They wanted us to have different hair.

1180
00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:39.020
Yeah.

1181
00:49:39.120 --> 00:49:40.640
I had to have different hair.

1182
00:49:41.060 --> 00:49:44.500
I sat at the bar with Jean.

1183
00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:45.900
And Lee.

1184
00:49:46.100 --> 00:49:46.560
And Lee.

1185
00:49:46.740 --> 00:49:47.040
Yeah.

1186
00:49:47.320 --> 00:49:51.340
Because they were in a—I remember they positioned us a little bit by height.

1187
00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:51.720
Yeah.

1188
00:49:51.960 --> 00:49:54.960
So I was in the front because I'm shorty and shorty.

1189
00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:55.010
Right.

1190
00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:58.040
They went shorter and then taller and so on and so forth.

1191
00:49:58.040 --> 00:50:01.080
And, you know, Threat Level Midnight was another BJ Novak script.

1192
00:50:01.300 --> 00:50:01.740
Mm-hmm.

1193
00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:04.280
So he really did bring you back a lot.

1194
00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:08.100
Again, he wrote me in literally once a season.

1195
00:50:08.780 --> 00:50:09.900
Oh, that's great.

1196
00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:16.580
So if it wasn't for him and Mindy, I would not have been in, like, almost every season.

1197
00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:17.240
Wow.

1198
00:50:17.440 --> 00:50:17.560
Yeah.

1199
00:50:17.760 --> 00:50:21.380
And I always—whenever I came back, I always said, thank you for writing me in.

1200
00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:22.260
He said, my pleasure.

1201
00:50:22.500 --> 00:50:24.780
And it was literally, like, once a season.

1202
00:50:25.260 --> 00:50:25.960
That's great.

1203
00:50:26.020 --> 00:50:26.240
Yeah.

1204
00:50:26.240 --> 00:50:26.290
Yeah.

1205
00:50:26.600 --> 00:50:30.960
I mean, we've definitely noticed over the years of re-watching the show themes that

1206
00:50:30.960 --> 00:50:33.900
writers were drawn to some characters and would bring them back.

1207
00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:34.820
So I love that.

1208
00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:36.440
I'm incredibly grateful.

1209
00:50:37.120 --> 00:50:37.600
All right.

1210
00:50:37.680 --> 00:50:39.280
Well, listen, why don't we take a break?

1211
00:50:39.460 --> 00:50:42.000
And then when we come back, we're going to ask you our call sheet questions.

1212
00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:45.140
And we're going to ask you our office question of the week.

1213
00:50:55.240 --> 00:50:55.780
All right.

1214
00:50:55.780 --> 00:50:58.460
We are back, and it is time for the call sheet questions.

1215
00:50:58.620 --> 00:50:59.960
I'm going to kick us off.

1216
00:51:00.160 --> 00:51:00.360
Okay.

1217
00:51:00.600 --> 00:51:01.580
With number one.

1218
00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:01.900
Yeah.

1219
00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:05.260
Carly, what was your first entertainment job?

1220
00:51:05.260 --> 00:51:13.300
The first official entertainment job was my friend, John Putch, hired me for my first

1221
00:51:13.300 --> 00:51:17.660
Hallmark movie, and it was with Louise Fletcher.

1222
00:51:18.080 --> 00:51:18.800
Whoa.

1223
00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:20.320
Oh, oh, oh.

1224
00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:23.520
I mean, and Doris Roberts.

1225
00:51:23.880 --> 00:51:24.340
Wow.

1226
00:51:24.640 --> 00:51:26.340
And Dana Delaney.

1227
00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:27.520
Wow.

1228
00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:32.000
And it was a Hallmark picture called A Time to Remember.

1229
00:51:32.340 --> 00:51:34.920
It was also with my friend, Michael Dean Jacobs.

1230
00:51:36.160 --> 00:51:41.620
Literally, like a direct hire, like John wanted to hire me, and I played Cousin Connie.

1231
00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:42.880
Cousin Connie?

1232
00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:43.580
Cousin Connie.

1233
00:51:44.060 --> 00:51:52.780
Doris Roberts was Dana Delaney's mom who had Alzheimer's, and Louise Fletcher played Aunt

1234
00:51:52.780 --> 00:51:53.100
Billie.

1235
00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:58.320
And it was a Thanksgiving movie called A Time to Remember, and it plays literally on Hallmark

1236
00:51:58.320 --> 00:51:59.100
every Thanksgiving.

1237
00:51:59.100 --> 00:52:00.000
Oh, no way.

1238
00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:00.360
Yeah.

1239
00:52:00.560 --> 00:52:02.880
And that was my first big job.

1240
00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:09.640
And I was like on set for like three days, and I just looked at him, and I said, you sat

1241
00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:14.160
me next to Louise Fletcher at a Thanksgiving table, and he says, just enjoy the ride.

1242
00:52:14.780 --> 00:52:18.760
And I was like, I'm sitting next to Nurse Ratched.

1243
00:52:19.140 --> 00:52:19.520
Yeah.

1244
00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:20.100
Ah, amazing.

1245
00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:21.560
That's Nurse Ratched.

1246
00:52:22.780 --> 00:52:23.440
All right.

1247
00:52:23.660 --> 00:52:24.920
Question number two.

1248
00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:26.980
Do you speak any other languages?

1249
00:52:27.440 --> 00:52:33.060
I speak French, sort of, because I took it in junior high.

1250
00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:34.500
So, un peu.

1251
00:52:34.740 --> 00:52:36.040
Un peu de français.

1252
00:52:36.240 --> 00:52:37.520
Un peu de français, oui.

1253
00:52:38.380 --> 00:52:39.500
Sounds good to me.

1254
00:52:39.640 --> 00:52:43.540
It got me through living in Paris for a month.

1255
00:52:44.160 --> 00:52:45.240
That's pretty good.

1256
00:52:45.440 --> 00:52:51.400
Because I was with the touring company of Hair when I was in New York.

1257
00:52:51.400 --> 00:52:56.040
I auditioned for it when I was training in New York, and I got the international touring

1258
00:52:56.040 --> 00:52:56.780
company of Hair.

1259
00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:58.160
That's amazing.

1260
00:52:58.780 --> 00:53:00.000
So, you did Hair?

1261
00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:00.340
Yeah.

1262
00:53:01.060 --> 00:53:01.340
Okay.

1263
00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:03.380
You know what Hair is famous for.

1264
00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:03.960
Yes, I do.

1265
00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:05.360
We all know how that goes at the end.

1266
00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:06.180
Did you do it?

1267
00:53:06.300 --> 00:53:11.760
I did, because I was in Paris, and nobody in Paris knew me, and they all used to seeing

1268
00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:14.620
naked people on the stage, so nobody knew me, so.

1269
00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:16.060
Wow.

1270
00:53:16.640 --> 00:53:17.900
I was a lot younger.

1271
00:53:18.020 --> 00:53:18.900
That was 1984.

